---
/**
 * One card on a section landing — the chooser tile (M4).
 *
 * ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * ⚠️ A CHOOSER THAT ONLY LISTS NAMES WASTES THE SCREEN IT COSTS.
 *
 * Adding a landing screen between the bar and the content is a whole extra tap
 * for every reader, every time. It has to pay for itself, and a stack of
 * bare links does not: that is a menu, and the bar was already a menu. Each
 * card therefore carries three things — the name, one line saying what is
 * behind it, and the reader's own state where any exists.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE STATE LINE IS THE PART THAT EARNS THE TAP. "8 sur 24 exercices
 * résolus" is the answer to the question the reader actually had; the name
 * alone is something they could already see in the bar.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE WHOLE CARD IS THE TARGET, not the title inside it. Anything smaller
 * on a phone means aiming, and a chooser you have to aim at is worse than the
 * list it replaced. The heading holds the link so the accessible name is the
 * card's name and screen-reader users get a real heading structure; the
 * `::after` overlay is what makes the rest of the block clickable.
 *
 * ⚠️ NO `href` MEANS NO CARD. Critical Feature 32 — an index card that renders
 * has a destination — applies here for the same reason: an inert tile tells a
 * reader the site is broken, and nothing is missing for a test to notice.
 * `href` is required by the type, and callers omit the whole card instead.
 * ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 */
interface Props {
  href: string;
  name: string;
  body: string;
  /** `data-testid`, so specs can address a card by what it is. */
  testid: string;
  /**
   * A journey id `ResumeResolver` is resolving on this page.
   *
   * ⚠️ THE RESOLVER OWNS THE READING, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `mcc:progress:v1`
   * is already read by the theme head script, `AccountButton`, the home
   * resolver and `/progres/`; a fifth copy here — on a page whose whole job is
   * to say how far along you are — is exactly how two surfaces come to disagree
   * about what a reader has done. This card only supplies the mount.
   *
   * ⚠️⚠️ IT ASKS FOR A COUNT AND NOTHING ELSE — SEE `data-resume-keep-href`
   * BELOW. Until v0.25.0 declaring a journey ALSO handed the resolver this
   * card's `href`, so "Ma progression" on `/moi/` pointed at the next unsolved
   * exercise instead of `/progres/`. A chooser card goes where it says
   * (Critical Feature 65); resuming belongs to the resume surfaces.
   */
  journey?: string | undefined;
  /** The `%s of %s` sentence for this journey, from the i18n table. */
  countPattern?: string | undefined;
  /** A plain fact, for a card with nothing trackable behind it. See the header. */
  fact?: string | undefined;
}

const { href, name, body, testid, journey, countPattern, fact } = Astro.props;
---

<li
  class="hub-card"
  data-testid={testid}
  data-resume-for={journey}
  data-resume-count-pattern={countPattern}
  data-resume-keep-href
>
  <h2 class="hub-card-name">
    <a href={href}>{name}</a>
  </h2>
  <p class="hub-card-body">{body}</p>
  {/* ⚠️ RESERVED, NOT CONDITIONAL. The tally is written by `ResumeResolver`
      after first paint; rendering the line only once a count exists would make
      every card jump the moment the script runs. It reserves its height and
      stays empty until there is something true to put in it — the same rule the
      solved marker on the index cards follows. */}
  {
    journey ? (
      <p class="hub-card-state">
        <span data-resume-count />
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p class="hub-card-state">{fact}</p>
    )
  }
</li>

<style>
  .hub-card {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    /* Generous, because this is the whole content of the screen — and it is the
       padding that makes the card a comfortable thumb target rather than a
       heading with text under it. */
    padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--mcc-border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    background-color: var(--mcc-surface-raised);
    /* Réponse (E1). Border and background only — a card that moves on hover
       reads as a drag handle. */
    transition:
      border-color var(--motion-response) var(--ease-response),
      background-color var(--motion-response) var(--ease-response);
  }

  .hub-card:hover,
  .hub-card:focus-within {
    border-color: var(--mcc-accent-strong);
  }

  .hub-card-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .hub-card-name a {
    color: var(--mcc-text-primary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* The overlay that makes the whole card the target. `inset: 0` on the card's
     own positioning context, so it covers the padding too. */
  .hub-card-name a::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  }

  .hub-card-name a:focus-visible::after {
    outline: 2px solid var(--mcc-focus-ring);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* ⚠️ NO `opacity` ON TEXT OVER AN AUDITED FILL — `check-contrast.mjs` proves
     the token pair and cannot see an alpha laid over it. Differentiated by
     size and by its own audited token. */
  .hub-card-body {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--mcc-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* Reserved whether or not it has content, so revealing it cannot reflow the
     grid — the same rule the index cards' solved marker follows. */
  .hub-card-state {
    min-block-size: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
    color: var(--mcc-accent-text);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .hub-card:active {
    transform: translateY(var(--motion-press-y));
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hub-card {
      transition: none;
    }

    .hub-card:active {
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
